{
  "id": "cpp_number_systems",
  "title": "Binary and hexadecimal number systems",
  "difficulty": "medium",
  "estimatedMinutes": 12,
  "sources": [
    "https://en.cppreference.com/w/cpp/language/integer_literal.html",
    "https://www.arduino.cc/reference/en/"
  ],
  "spans": [
    {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "Everyday counting uses decimal, whose place weights are powers of 10. Binary uses only ",
      "size": 16
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "0",
      "size": 16,
      "bold": true,
      "color": "#1565C0"
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "text": " and ",
      "size": 16
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "1",
      "size": 16,
      "bold": true,
      "color": "#1565C0"
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "; from the right, place weights are 1, 2, 4, 8, 16. For example, ",
      "size": 16
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "1101₂ = 8 + 4 + 0 + 1 = 13₁₀",
      "size": 16,
      "bold": true,
      "color": "#1565C0"
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "text": ". Hexadecimal uses 16 symbols, ",
      "size": 16
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "0–9",
      "size": 16,
      "bold": true,
      "color": "#1565C0"
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "text": " and ",
      "size": 16
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "A–F",
      "size": 16,
      "bold": true,
      "color": "#1565C0"
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "; one hex digit represents exactly four bits. ",
      "size": 16
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "0x3A = 3×16 + 10 = 58",
      "size": 16,
      "bold": true,
      "color": "#1565C0"
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "text": ", or ",
      "size": 16
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "0011 1010",
      "size": 16,
      "bold": true,
      "color": "#1565C0"
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "text": " in binary.\n\nOn microcontrollers, hexadecimal makes long bit patterns shorter and easier to inspect in registers, bit masks, memory addresses, and sensor dumps. ",
      "size": 16
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "0b...",
      "size": 16,
      "bold": true,
      "color": "#1565C0"
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "text": " is a binary literal and ",
      "size": 16
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "0x...",
      "size": 16,
      "bold": true,
      "color": "#1565C0"
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "text": " is hexadecimal; ",
      "size": 16
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "uint8_t",
      "size": 16,
      "bold": true,
      "color": "#1565C0"
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "text": " stores an 8-bit value from 0 to 255.\n\n",
      "size": 16
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "CONVERSIONS, STEP BY STEP\n\nDecimal to binary: divide 13 by 2 repeatedly: 13÷2=6 remainder 1; 6÷2=3 remainder 0; 3÷2=1 remainder 1; 1÷2=0 remainder 1. Read the remainders bottom to top: ",
      "size": 16
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "1101₂",
      "size": 16,
      "bold": true,
      "color": "#1565C0"
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "text": ". Check: ",
      "size": 16
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "8+4+1=13",
      "size": 16,
      "bold": true,
      "color": "#1565C0"
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "text": ".\n\nBinary to decimal: multiply each digit by its place weight and add: ",
      "size": 16
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "101101₂ = 1×32 + 0×16 + 1×8 + 1×4 + 0×2 + 1×1 = 45₁₀",
      "size": 16,
      "bold": true,
      "color": "#1565C0"
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "text": ".\n\nDecimal to hex: 58÷16=3 remainder 10; the hexadecimal symbol for 10 is ",
      "size": 16
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "A",
      "size": 16,
      "bold": true,
      "color": "#1565C0"
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "text": ", so the answer is ",
      "size": 16
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "3A₁₆",
      "size": 16,
      "bold": true,
      "color": "#1565C0"
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "text": ", or ",
      "size": 16
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "0x3A",
      "size": 16,
      "bold": true,
      "color": "#1565C0"
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "text": ". Hex to decimal: ",
      "size": 16
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "3A₁₆ = 3×16 + 10×1 = 58₁₀",
      "size": 16,
      "bold": true,
      "color": "#1565C0"
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "text": ".\n\nBinary to hex: group bits into fours from the right: ",
      "size": 16
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "00111010 → 0011 1010 → 3A",
      "size": 16,
      "bold": true,
      "color": "#1565C0"
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "text": ". Hex to binary: replace each digit with its four-bit row: ",
      "size": 16
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "2D → 0010 1101",
      "size": 16,
      "bold": true,
      "color": "#1565C0"
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "text": ". Leading zeroes only complete a group; they do not change the value.\n\nCHECK YOURSELF: ",
      "size": 16
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "1111₂ = 15₁₀ = F₁₆",
      "size": 16,
      "bold": true,
      "color": "#1565C0"
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "; ",
      "size": 16
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "2A₁₆ = 42₁₀ = 0010 1010₂",
      "size": 16,
      "bold": true,
      "color": "#1565C0"
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "text": ". The ",
      "size": 16
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "0x",
      "size": 16,
      "bold": true,
      "color": "#1565C0"
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "text": " prefix only marks a hexadecimal literal. These conversions help read code, sensor data, and bit masks.\n\n",
      "size": 16
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "Binary, decimal, and hexadecimal digit map\n",
      "size": 17,
      "bold": true
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "text",
      "code": "Binary | Decimal | Hex\n0000 | 0  | 0\n0001 | 1  | 1\n0010 | 2  | 2\n0011 | 3  | 3\n0100 | 4  | 4\n0101 | 5  | 5\n0110 | 6  | 6\n0111 | 7  | 7\n1000 | 8  | 8\n1001 | 9  | 9\n1010 | 10 | A\n1011 | 11 | B\n1100 | 12 | C\n1101 | 13 | D\n1110 | 14 | E\n1111 | 15 | F",
      "showCopyButton": true
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "cpp",
      "code": "// Decimal 58 = 0011 1010 (binary) = 0x3A (hex)\nuint8_t value = 0b00111010;\nuint8_t sameValue = 0x3A;\n\nuint8_t flags = 0;\nflags |= (1u << 2);  // Set bit 2\nbool enabled = (flags & (1u << 2)) != 0;\nflags = static_cast<uint8_t>(flags & ~(1u << 2)); // Clear bit 2",
      "showCopyButton": true
    },
    {
      "type": "warning",
      "title": "Important note",
      "text": "To convert binary to decimal, add the place weights for each `1`. To convert decimal to binary, repeatedly divide by 2 and read remainders in reverse. Bit positions start at 0. Check shift width and target type; use the board's reference manual before writing hardware registers."
    }
  ],
